course: dictionary
lesson: 5362
title: Kinship: family and feeling
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Kinship means being related to someone by family, through birth or through marriage. «Kinship carers love the children they care for, and they can get tired» means a grandparent or other relative is looking after a child.
explain: Kinship can also mean a close feeling for someone because you share something, like interests or troubles. «She felt a kinship with the lonely boy» tells you she understood how he felt, because the clues are «felt» and «lonely».
explain: To check your meaning, ask whether the sentence is about family or about feelings. Then put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Kinship means being related to someone by family, by birth or by marriage.
fact: Kinship can also mean a feeling of being close to someone because you share interests or troubles.
fact: Kinship care is when a child is looked after by a grandparent or another relative.
fact: «Some kinship care is made official», so the law can recognise it.
fact: «Kinship carers often need help, and that's fine.» Children can help, and they should tell a trusted adult if they are worried.
exercise: «Kinship carers love the children they care for, and they can get tired.» What is a kinship carer? | *A grandparent or other relative who looks after a child | A child who has no family at all | A doctor who checks children at school
exercise: «She felt a kinship with the lonely boy.» What does kinship mean here? | A fight over a toy | *A close feeling, because they share something | Being related to the boy by birth
exercise: «Kinship care can turn cousins into brothers and sisters.» Which words show that the cousins become family? | care | cousins | *brothers and sisters | can
test: What is the main meaning of kinship? | A type of sport played in schools | A large house in the country | Being shy with strangers | *Being related to someone by family, by birth or marriage
test: Which sentence uses kinship to mean a close feeling? | Some kinship care is made official | Lexi's auntie gets a letter | Kinship carers love the children they care for | *She felt a kinship with the lonely boy
exam: Mia says kinship care means a child lives in a children's home run by strangers. Is she right? | No, it means a child is never looked after by anyone | Yes, strangers always run kinship care | *No, it means a child is looked after by a grandparent or other relative | Yes, but only in schools
